WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.670
So without further ado.

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.718
Here is Conan O'Brien.

00:00:04.718 --> 00:00:06.313
(host clapping)

00:00:06.313 --> 00:00:09.813
(gentle orchestral music)

00:00:15.342 --> 00:00:19.259
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:00:51.902 --> 00:00:52.811
Thank you.

00:00:52.811 --> 00:00:55.780
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.290
Okay, come on, okay, please.
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.507
Thank you, no, that's too much (laughs).

00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:06.510
I haven't even started yet (laughs).

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.237
Thank you, thank, whoa, oh my God.

00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:12.981
That's crazy!
(plane engines roaring)

00:01:12.981 --> 00:01:15.877
(cheering and engines drown out Conan)

00:01:15.877 --> 00:01:18.343
Oh man, thank you, yeah, whoa!

00:01:18.343 --> 00:01:19.550
(artillery fires)

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.010
All right, thank you,
thank you please, please.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:24.843
Let's begin.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:32.190
Greetings graduates,
faculty, parents, relatives,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.580
Zoom bombers, and the true heroes today,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.320
the Harvard IT Department.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.690
Really nice compression, guys.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.410
Beautiful, very little buffering.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.310
Nice job nerds.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.980
Welcome to Harvard's
first online graduation,

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:52.754
and congratulations to
the Harvard Class of 2020.

00:01:52.754 --> 00:01:54.163
(claps) Yes.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.620
I wanna begin by telling
you that the administration

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.150
has given me only 10
minutes to speak today,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.793
instead of the traditional 20.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.620
I promised to do my best, since everyone's

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.620
in such a big hurry to get back
to doing absolutely nothing!

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.670
I'd like to take just a moment
to thank Harvard College

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.400
for bestowing upon me an honorary degree,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.160
Doctorate of Humane
Letters and Philosophy.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.420
I am truly, truly honored.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.020
I'd also like to thank Harvard

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.360
for a doctorate honoring
my groundbreaking work

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.036
in both sonic string theory
and condensed matter physics.

00:02:41.036 --> 00:02:43.780
Thank you, thank you, I
worked with a great team,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:45.170
thank you all.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.410
Seniors, I know you belong

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.890
in the Tercentenary Theatre right now,

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.120
believe me all of us miss Harvard Yard.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.740
I mean, where else can you find

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.740
two competing pro-communist newsletters

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.470
written in Dothraki all
while signing a petition

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.090
to ban hotdogs and donuts

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.010
for promoting cisgender identities.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.130
What a rare and unusual place.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:09.140
But trust me, we are taking steps

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:13.000
to make today's commencement
feel as authentic as possible.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.700
In fact, right now, Harvard
is charging each of you $50

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.580
for parking in Cambridge.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.260
Yeah, plus, Harvard will see to it

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.620
that within the next hour,
one of your grandparents

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.223
will be hit in the head
with a stray Frisbee.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:30.430
And be assured, one day in the future,

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.040
you will return to your
alma mater, but for now,

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.670
you get to enjoy me speaking
to you from my backyard.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.940
Harvard has been graduating
students for nearly 400 years.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.370
This is the first one

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.870
where the commencement
speaker is standing on a spot

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.600
where his dog urinated seven minutes ago.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.630
Right over here.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.520
This area, yes.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.550
Yes, this commencement
will be very different.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:59.550
As you sit here today, or
stand, or microwave a burrito,

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.120
or ride a Peloton, or
recline uncomfortably

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.070
in your childhood bed, or mine Bitcoin,

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.000
or Google who is Conan O'Brien,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.000
you are witnessing many, many
firsts in today's ceremony.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:18.870
For example, normally, graduates
would greet and shake hands

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.860
with their faculty deans,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.380
but now that shaking
hands is a Class D Felony,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:25.850
those faculty members decided instead

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:28.893
to give you a virtual butt bump on TikTok.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.050
Yeah, and by the way, congratulations

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.470
to the department heads,

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.863
you really captured what
Cotton Mather was all about.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.030
In another first,

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.490
this year Harvard has
discarded traditional grading

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.030
and switched to a pass/fail system.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:46.810
That's right, as a result,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.193
several members of the
lacrosse team will graduate.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:53.540
And finally, for the first time,

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.250
all of you will receive
your diplomas by mail.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.470
Now to make sure those diplomas

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.360
are not stolen from your
porch by package thieves,

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.950
they will be mailed to you
in a plain brown wrapper

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.823
labeled Cornell University Diploma.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:08.330
It's foolproof (laughs).

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.357
No one's touching that thing (laughs).

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.920
Now I know for all of you,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.010
this is not the graduation
ceremony you were expecting.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.660
Right now, your parents are realizing

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.900
they spent hundreds of
thousands of dollars

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.060
just to watch you basically graduate

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.140
from an online university.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.650
In fact, this speech is
being simulcast right now

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.830
to the University of Phoenix.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.345
Yeah! (laughs)

00:05:33.345 --> 00:05:36.720
(orchestral music)
Phoenix, Phoenix!

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.460
Phoenix gets it done, yeah!

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.272
They get it done in two
years, not four, uh!

00:05:41.272 --> 00:05:44.189
(orchestral music)

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:52.490
Anyway, I know what some of
you are wondering right now.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:56.260
How could Conan, as his
age, possibly relate to me?

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.010
Well to you I say, do not think of me

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:01.290
as someone who graduated
Harvard 35 years ago.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.460
Think of me as a fellow classmate,

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.550
a fellow classmate who looks like shit.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:09.040
Somehow kind of wrinkled and
bloated at the same time.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.610
Very hard to do.

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:14.120
You see, I have some experience
as a graduation speaker.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.840
20 years ago, I told the
graduating Harvard Students

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.970
to break out of their
cocoon and take chances.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.530
Well, 20 years later, I'd
like to amend that slightly

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.950
and say, stay in your cocoon!

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:28.950
Stay, the cocoon, stay in it.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.570
I had no idea about the virus.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.420
And to you students who've
moved back home to a blue state,

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.320
please continue to be patient,
wear a face mask outside,

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:39.170
and trust in science.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:42.490
To those of you who moved
back home to a red state,

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.573
I'll see you guys tonight at Applebee's!

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.750
Get those onion rings!

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:51.940
So do not despair this online
ceremony, but celebrate it.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:54.090
You have been handed two choices.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.120
Use the trial of this global pandemic

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.330
as the inspirational launching
pad to a greater destiny,

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:04.140
or two, go the Trump route,
and blame all your problems

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.410
on the previous graduating class.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.670
Come on, who's with me,
damn you class of 2019,

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:11.963
damn you to hell.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:16.200
If nothing else, just make that a t-shirt.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.580
Damn you to hell, 2019, that's a seller,

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:21.653
that moves, that's merch that moves.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:26.890
I realize I've said many
foolish things to you today.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.080
It's kind of what I do.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.420
But I would like to be sincere.

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:35.450
Before I spoke today,
I really did give you,

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:38.280
the Class of 2020, serious thought.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.000
And I'm honestly stunned

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.020
by what you've endured
in your short lives.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:47.570
For my Harvard class,
9/11 came in midlife.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.600
But I was thinking about it,
you came into consciousness

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:54.040
while our nation was still
smoldering from that attack,

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.430
and you've only known a world
beset by terrorist hate.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.560
You've grown up with mass
shootings and school lockdowns,

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.973
horrors completely
absent from my childhood.

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:07.360
You have now witnessed
two economic meltdowns

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.840
of stunning proportions.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.720
You are actually living in a hotter

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.580
and more treacherous biosphere
than the one I was born into.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.090
Two months into your freshman year,

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:19.870
many of you voted for the first
time in the 2016 election,

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.710
and ever since, you've been living

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:25.133
in the most polarized United
States since the Civil War.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:29.770
Now this you know all too well.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:32.340
You've been handed more than your share.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.610
Tom Brokaw famously hailed the people

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.200
who survived the Great
Depression and World War Two,

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.200
not because of who they were,
as much as what they endured.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.880
He called them the greatest generation.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.330
Now we don't know what you will be called

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:49.650
because the generation
that cleaned up the mess

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.850
left by those a-hole boomers

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.603
doesn't just roll off the tongue.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:57.550
You've been challenged your entire lives,

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:58.760
and you've demonstrated

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:02.150
one of the most precious
qualities one can have.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:03.670
Resilience.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:06.440
Now cynics like to mock
the supposedly spoiled

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.150
or callow youth of the new millennia.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.630
But you have seen and survived so much.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:15.740
And you've responded with wit,
creativity, righteous anger,

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.940
activism, and a gritty determination

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:22.010
to take the reality you've
been handed and make it better.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:23.730
Look at you, 10 weeks ago,

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:26.560
you were told to leave
your dorms immediately,

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.500
get your stuff, get out,

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:31.610
something no one has said to
me since my first marriage.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:35.150
And since that day, you
have found ways to stay safe

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.270
under absurd and confusing circumstances.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:41.370
And one way or the other,
your hard won resilience

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:44.980
has gotten you here, today,
to your TV, or phone,

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:46.950
or tablet, or smartwatch,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:50.883
to graduate from Harvard
come hell or high water.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:53.960
As the father of two teenage children,

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:57.280
I look at all you've achieved
in a frightful world,

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.290
and it gives me great hope.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:02.630
You are remarkable examples to my children

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.850
of how to be smart, brave,

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.083
and yes, resilient in a scary world.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.810
My generation was always embarrassed

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:14.160
to admit we went to Harvard,
for fear of bragging.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:17.220
But you, class of 2020,
have earned the right

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.400
to be proud of your great accomplishment.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.340
The next time someone asks
you where you went to college,

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.140
hold your head high, look
'em square in the eye

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:29.153
and say two miles south of Tufts.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.990
So today is unusual in two respects.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:36.870
First, as I've said,

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:40.540
this iconic 384-year-old
ceremony is online.

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:42.960
And we are strangely separate on a day

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.183
that's supposed to embody community.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:49.350
Second, and more
important, today's unusual

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:53.523
because at this commencement,
you commence nothing.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:57.480
You and your uniquely tested peers,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.020
your generation commenced long ago

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.770
while the rest of us were busy complaining

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:04.293
about the Sopranos finale.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:08.970
I have nothing to give you
that you don't already have.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.640
And have had for a very long time.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:15.250
What I can offer is my
boundless admiration,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:19.100
my deepest respect, my
gratitude for working so hard,

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.730
and bringing such honor to your families,

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:23.913
your faculty, and your alumni.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:26.870
Our current troubles will pass,

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:31.020
and you will laugh and thrive
and experience great joy.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:34.790
I cannot wait to see what you
fine people will accomplish,

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:37.860
and I know that one day, years from now,

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:42.860
one of you will stand where
I stand, here in my backyard.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:47.433
And I will have you
arrested for trespassing.

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:49.643
Thank you.

00:11:49.643 --> 00:11:54.641
(orchestral music)
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:11:56.105 --> 00:11:57.163
Thank you.

00:11:57.163 --> 00:12:02.163
(orchestral music)
(audience cheers and applauds)

